<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#dauphin-of-france">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dauphin of France</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dauphin of Viennois</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dauphin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> was the title of the heir apparent to the French throne from 1350 to 1830. It derived from the Dauphins of Viennois, rulers of the Dauphiné, whose last count, Humbert II, sold his lands to France on condition that they pass to the king's eldest son. The first royal Dauphin was the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>, and the Dauphiné was governed as the heir's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> until it was merged into the kingdom.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dauphin-of-france.jpg" alt="Dauphin of France" width="320" height="409" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sodacan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_Arms_of_the_Dauphin_of_France.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
